#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e69a4aee-6e92-598b-99b5-1d830b6dea54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Statements About a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af9aa7f-a7bf-5d4a-a8bd-bb821d3e7b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259975} {:answer/id #uuid "28d8ac93-4b75-5896-9a82-f0496d3ca6e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260235} {:answer/id #uuid "2de84256-eda7-51b0-9e39-5ddd3f1fe62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260288} {:answer/id #uuid "8938a01a-ae24-581d-82c9-76cbbf2080d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261069} {:answer/id #uuid "a0565217-a3d6-5235-85e3-63961a7fa4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0565217-a3d6-5235-85e3-63961a7fa4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261266} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b8d013-3023-5389-8092-2613be14d1b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a03e6ad4-897b-5475-96e7-f211a321e037" :question/math-academy-id "q-126484" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is upper triangular\n- $B$ is lower triangular\n- ${B}^{T}$ is lower triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $B$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. All the entries above the main diagonal of $B$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false, since $B$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n{B}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d33d3f-abc9-5dc0-b944-3762fb6bba3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260181} {:answer/id #uuid "40cc6a29-fd71-533a-9a9d-34ccbe2f0996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260442} {:answer/id #uuid "56332bab-2973-555d-ab2c-56ec7782b677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260613} {:answer/id #uuid "72638b52-a9e8-5fbc-8c2c-293738ed41fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260874} {:answer/id #uuid "e461fc12-b1cc-5c6a-91d7-2643815a3b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e461fc12-b1cc-5c6a-91d7-2643815a3b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261882} :answer-field/id #uuid "7646d55d-316c-51d4-9ae3-2d4a26a7ba3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f86fe2f1-042a-5183-8a18-518a658cc1a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128474" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\n- $det(Q)=-5$\n- $det(Q)=1$\n- $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(9-5)-1(6-3)-1(10-9) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-3-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6572756-5dae-592a-91b5-e4d9c44dc24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261322} {:answer/id #uuid "ae54066a-60f7-51af-9234-5cf17af6ca58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261414} {:answer/id #uuid "b49a3fe1-eabb-57c3-a213-d17f033ae81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261465} {:answer/id #uuid "ce73cc3c-f3b7-5235-aab7-7491eb71d6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261701} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d1de5d-a183-5f5d-9765-0a848886eaa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6572756-5dae-592a-91b5-e4d9c44dc24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261322} :answer-field/id #uuid "94562ada-6c89-5e49-865f-07d574285d9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "898815e0-65a8-55f9-b81f-a64efbb5e7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-128476" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\n- $T$ is a singular matrix\n- $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- $det(T)=-30$\n\n \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1-3)-3(2-1)-5(6-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2-3-25 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cec6e86-8f96-50d0-a127-4c77a072ced0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259996} {:answer/id #uuid "1f110755-d802-5805-a487-74fab27210e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260142} {:answer/id #uuid "a86f4d72-db21-5e9b-aca3-e0b71a9af2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261344} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3cbf1b-0f66-5a8a-9421-24a45bf27f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261697} {:answer/id #uuid "d08bb68f-9123-54c4-a315-f83db9f75c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f110755-d802-5805-a487-74fab27210e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260142} :answer-field/id #uuid "2095097c-7ca9-597d-847f-34889e65fa0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0d8a3d8-8c1e-55f4-b495-022734d0f802" :question/math-academy-id "q-128484" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\n- $R$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(R)=0$\n- $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ba8325-0de5-55e0-b591-b13e3cdf4ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263214} {:answer/id #uuid "9a082c1d-14e4-53ff-92d2-66ed283dd413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264320} {:answer/id #uuid "9fad71dc-b40f-5c4c-bb22-8e3334af0c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264360} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e9170b-f2b7-5b98-8476-275056448f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264711} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d0c06d-23f7-52e0-b017-3b762a830f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fad71dc-b40f-5c4c-bb22-8e3334af0c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264360} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5cca5b-3f01-52ef-89bb-4b92a787b6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48d2c508-8f65-5677-91ea-4bbc2f84d34b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131192" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e30919dc7cc107bdda9872079b4dc0e59fe8e3e5767aa2d407b9d0af392803e0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e3d2fa-2512-5462-9398-2a405f6d7d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263317} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0b785a-0eb8-57a0-825a-938a683bad1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263776} {:answer/id #uuid "8820c9da-a52e-5b7c-8e1b-89811eeef148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264175} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9b9b35-f09b-5b88-9a8b-3096512d0b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264185} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a018d7-e9e5-5ca8-9567-308f5666fcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a0b785a-0eb8-57a0-825a-938a683bad1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263776} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d7511c4-3f39-553d-b99b-12a596c766ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4805821b-99fd-5b9d-aaa1-3579778dee2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131193" :question/problem "![](images/24/243496443c3878c90fb67888add6c6ad6d3e69aa20ba24f42ee89d58a2742534.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c1aead-eb9d-582c-b0d6-f68d1170a78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263474} {:answer/id #uuid "6af6b5a3-6222-5cfd-8bca-6b4614394bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263920} {:answer/id #uuid "716bd08d-5f1f-5c82-bf30-535c8d7dc64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263980} {:answer/id #uuid "b2edc390-c4fb-5a29-a137-faed40c674b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264537} {:answer/id #uuid "bce50868-fc30-5b0e-b83f-795ca49eb4e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce50868-fc30-5b0e-b83f-795ca49eb4e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264638} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e85999-ff43-5a2b-9df8-c9be1c0d7380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a88235e-0bbc-5c38-af1d-92f6a6eda26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131361" :question/problem "![](images/17/1709a94b7361a1e8764d94199f9e84802ae925a09e6481250d0846b83bb6549f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/7c/7c831425eae7afd709d546a8e05100f495adc04bba6450cfa56d370b415a28f5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063a878d-5e77-56b6-a73e-b63e1d49fa39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263056} {:answer/id #uuid "16b6734b-5505-5646-a97a-a8ee2a8ba3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263213} {:answer/id #uuid "1740b55b-0519-5f5d-a189-ef0c5a09b842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263221} {:answer/id #uuid "19bcf365-570c-583d-9efd-87145dc67749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263246} {:answer/id #uuid "5b043794-cc9f-5600-8de5-98b1c61c7162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "063a878d-5e77-56b6-a73e-b63e1d49fa39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263056} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cec731f-7c57-5058-87bf-7218fda9c7e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f317d036-e0ac-5288-a673-4543ead19ce4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131366" :question/problem "![](images/78/781a4e33b8b55762b2dcb7575d08315b5842f83d6d78a5136a1c353eefd3069d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/97/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48dd525f-5280-5616-ad30-ba5ee70ca286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263627} {:answer/id #uuid "97fe1cbb-75ac-5ed4-8c6f-28135d822617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264306} {:answer/id #uuid "9866ac27-4d10-5e12-a5f2-20a090920029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264310} {:answer/id #uuid "a4052d0a-62cc-5175-8982-a3a60adc8f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264396} {:answer/id #uuid "f78545d9-aee6-50ba-a9e2-99bc93090923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48dd525f-5280-5616-ad30-ba5ee70ca286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263627} :answer-field/id #uuid "63032b45-ca95-5403-83e8-7551c5787d42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7e2bc11-b1bf-5938-9267-7923a80ec463" :question/math-academy-id "q-131719" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-1 \\\\  & 2x+8y=-2 \\\\  & -3x-12y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The system is inconsistent\n\nII. The system is consistent dependent\n\nIII. The system has a unique solution" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2373407a-5dc4-5aa0-9261-b3a83be143a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263310} {:answer/id #uuid "37e5535a-47db-5120-b920-f8711ded0365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263504} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4a05dd-9346-5c44-8eaf-7488e248aafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264090} {:answer/id #uuid "d7113481-5195-5749-9632-d40d020970ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264873} {:answer/id #uuid "f87a25e5-0907-579e-b6ce-cc2aa703c11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e4a05dd-9346-5c44-8eaf-7488e248aafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264090} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c27f11-9954-5e36-8021-98da12eda34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a61d7528-3ce2-5d0a-af23-11395074997a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131723" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y+z=3 \\\\  & -3x+3y-3z=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y+0z=19,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46470091-2ac4-5f59-adc5-0a311982854f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263616} {:answer/id #uuid "80d5d80a-e41d-5438-a1c7-07302fd289ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264107} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bfe79b-ea64-5a96-b000-a996d4345dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264709} {:answer/id #uuid "ce229d18-324f-56e3-9d1d-7ca1417d2702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264803} {:answer/id #uuid "d90f70d7-fb30-5e3a-a741-ee1cb5651f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3bfe79b-ea64-5a96-b000-a996d4345dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264709} :answer-field/id #uuid "7601e031-d2c6-5fed-a0f8-b45602534fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09e95d08-30a6-5777-a8a8-348db8bfab42" :question/math-academy-id "q-131724" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=2 \\\\  & -3x-4y=3 \\\\  & 2x-4y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is inconsistent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-5,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e69a4aee-6e92-598b-99b5-1d830b6dea54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Statements About a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12389f18-052b-519d-9a2a-b2d04cff0ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266299} {:answer/id #uuid "522bdf1e-83a7-5e64-a119-0ea3f50e57df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266877} {:answer/id #uuid "86da3a70-ff02-5856-bfe6-bfd52e0aa79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267335} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4628c9-3834-5295-8683-2196b83efb01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267376} {:answer/id #uuid "9368856a-1f6b-5077-95c9-62f7f1fdb0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "522bdf1e-83a7-5e64-a119-0ea3f50e57df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266877} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7383b10-7ab3-525c-8800-3eda85c0e909" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ccdf19a6-45a3-5b56-b00e-b00d25e501ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-13771" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is upper triangular\n\nII. $A$ is lower triangular\n\nIII. ${A}^{T}+A$ is lower triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since all the entries below the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true, since the result of $A+{A}^{T}$ has all the entries above the main diagonal equal to zero: \n\n$$\n{A}^{T}+A=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34270485-5bff-52bf-ae41-08b1bee1a1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269746} {:answer/id #uuid "389a0c65-5adf-5c0f-9d62-a0d6a6c0821b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269783} {:answer/id #uuid "5531b938-deac-5f0d-96bc-e4236e777318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270026} {:answer/id #uuid "8e825cdc-2aaf-56bb-9c28-3d7dfac2e941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270505} {:answer/id #uuid "d0121132-7103-5393-80a4-507acd8c4184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34270485-5bff-52bf-ae41-08b1bee1a1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1beb641a-e76c-5ac9-9803-42dc3f2e4a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c28aa3d-73cf-552e-9e05-dfc5bcb67151" :question/math-academy-id "q-141080" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & \\ne 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\ne \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65720cff-ac67-573c-b355-c946b6d47e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270173} {:answer/id #uuid "771372c6-f984-51d7-819d-583b075d5f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270320} {:answer/id #uuid "c019a28a-dcd7-5e5a-a2e2-15357e719fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270971} {:answer/id #uuid "c724b081-8081-5843-86e5-29e06d070d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271023} {:answer/id #uuid "de5a2a25-e529-5bdd-9c7e-749f5a48a47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c724b081-8081-5843-86e5-29e06d070d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271023} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e408fe-eb76-5085-b71a-96030d52904b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82065c87-2fb6-51b8-8d32-9c57d0719fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141081" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\ne {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcca0f3-a734-5821-a06d-f8343e70ea2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269534} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5dd004-13d5-581c-aad4-bee0a7e60ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270239} {:answer/id #uuid "73a31251-c78f-5484-b652-d7fc86f10971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270290} {:answer/id #uuid "93bacff5-8f98-5a78-9a0a-9604f419972e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270560} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2bf753-807f-5720-a657-2805a9668a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fcca0f3-a734-5821-a06d-f8343e70ea2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269534} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a79a925-e351-511b-b352-4a48c43fdc1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b7a88f7-a074-5f69-9224-f1585f849cc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141082" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ae0ac0-cbd7-5e19-9e62-18c4e30401ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270299} {:answer/id #uuid "7d855e8a-1d57-5875-8d6d-12d0951b8e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270369} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7151bb-1ac2-5705-85fc-5081794b458e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270380} {:answer/id #uuid "c08033e6-c809-5d66-be80-0f4095dc7570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270973} {:answer/id #uuid "f54e5a37-096a-5380-9441-b692b0890ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f54e5a37-096a-5380-9441-b692b0890ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271423} :answer-field/id #uuid "574997d4-2cf3-5549-972e-17ab6fe6132d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4402732-29b4-541b-a6e6-a372fd33bf97" :question/math-academy-id "q-141084" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}]}
